{At board no.1, Mr Hart-Dyke (the Cambridge blind player) opened with 1 P-Q4, to which Mr Lawton replied 1 P-K3, and the opening passed into a Queen's Gambit Declined. Mr Lawton was left with an isolated Queen's Pawn, and this Mr Hart-Dyke ultimately won, mainly though Mr Lawton pushing a premature attack.} 1. d4 e6 2. c4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 c5 5. dxc5 Bxc5 6. cxd5 exd5 7. Bg5 Qb6 8. e3 Ng4 9. Be2 h6 10. Bf4 g5 11. Bxb8 Rxb8 12. Bb5+ Kf8 13. Qxd5 a6 14. Be2 Bxe3 15. fxe3 Nxe3 16. Qe5 Nc2+ 17. Kd2 {Sources: London Evening Standard, 2 April 1895; Cambridge Independent Press, 5 April 1895; The Times, 8 April 1895; Chess Monthly, Vol. 16, p271} 1-0

